"Remarks on the conjectured log-Brunn-Minkowski inequality (https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.4954)\nciteturn1academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.9 years ago; Authors: Christos Saroglou ... Moreover, we prove that the log-Brunn-Minkowski inequality is equivalent to the (B)-Theorem for the uniform measure of the cube (this has been proven by Cordero-Erasquin, Fradelizi and Maurey for the gaussian measure instead).\nTitle: Remarks on the conjectured log-Brunn-Minkowski inequality\nAuthors: Christos Saroglou\nDate: Wed Nov 20 04:43:29 2013\n\n\\footnotesize B\\\"{o}r\\\"{o}czky, Lutwak, Yang and Zhang recently conjectured a certain strengthening of the Brunn-Minkowski inequality for symmetric convex bodies, the so-called log-Brunn-Minkowski inequality. We establish this inequality together with its equality cases for pairs of unconditional convex bodies with respect to some orthonormal basis. Applications of this fact are discussed. Moreover, we prove that the log-Brunn-Minkowski inequality is equivalent to the (B)-Theorem for the uniform measure of the cube (this has been proven by Cordero-Erasquin, Fradelizi and Maurey for the gaussian measure instead).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIsoperimetric Inequalities on Slabs with applications to Cubes and Gaussian Slabs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.06602)\nciteturn1academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.6 years ago; Title: Isoperimetric Inequalities on Slabs with applications to Cubes and Gaussian SlabsAuthors: Emanuel MilmanDate: Mon Mar 11 10:49:17 2024 ... Our analysis confirms the isoperimetric conjecture on the three-dimensional cube with side lengths $(\\beta,1,1)$ in a new range of relatives volumes $\\bar v \\in [0,1/2]$. ... In the case of a slab with a Gaussian base of width $T>0$, we identify a phase transition when $T = \\sqrt{2 \\pi}$ and when $T = \\pi$.\nTitle: Isoperimetric Inequalities on Slabs with applications to Cubes and Gaussian Slabs\nAuthors: Emanuel Milman\nDate: Mon Mar 11 10:49:17 2024\n\nWe study isoperimetric inequalities on \"slabs\", namely weighted Riemannian manifolds obtained as the product of the uniform measure on a finite length interval with a codimension-one base. As our two main applications, we consider the case when the base is the flat torus $\\mathbb{R}^2 / 2 \\mathbb{Z}^2$ and the standard Gaussian measure in $\\mathbb{R}^{n-1}$. The isoperimetric conjecture on the three-dimensional cube predicts that minimizers are enclosed by spheres about a corner, cylinders about an edge and coordinate planes. This has only been established for relative volumes close to $0$, $1/2$ and $1$ by compactness arguments. Our analysis confirms the isoperimetric conjecture on the three-dimensional cube with side lengths $(\\beta,1,1)$ in a new range of relatives volumes $\\bar v \\in [0,1/2]$. In particular, we confirm the conjecture for the standard cube ($\\beta=1$) for all $\\bar v \\leq 0.120582$, when $\\beta \\leq 0.919431$ for the entire range where spheres are conjectured to be minimizing, and also for all $\\bar v \\in [0,1/2] \\setminus (\\frac{1}{\\pi} - \\frac{\\beta}{4},\\frac{1}{\\pi} + \\frac{\\beta}{4})$. When $\\beta \\leq 0.919431$ we reduce the validity of the full conjecture to establishing that the half-plane $\\{ x \\in [0,\\beta] \\times [0,1]^2 \\; ; \\; x_3 \\leq \\frac{1}{\\pi} \\}$ is an isoperimetric minimizer. We also show that the analogous conjecture on a high-dimensional cube $[0,1]^n$ is false for $n \\geq 10$. In the case of a slab with a Gaussian base of width $T>0$, we identify a phase transition when $T = \\sqrt{2 \\pi}$ and when $T = \\pi$. In particular, while products of half-planes with $[0,T]$ are always minimizing when $T \\leq \\sqrt{2 \\pi}$, when $T > \\pi$ they are never minimizing, being beaten by Gaussian unduloids. In the range $T \\in (\\sqrt{2 \\pi},\\pi]$, a potential trichotomy occurs.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMore on logarithmic sums of convex bodies (https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.4346)\nciteturn1academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.1 years ago; Title: More on logarithmic sums of convex bodiesAuthors: Christos Saroglou ... We prove that the log-Brunn-Minkowski inequality (log-BMI) for the Lebesque measure in dimension $n$ would imply the log-BMI and, therefore, the B-conjecture for any log-concave density in dimension $n$.\nTitle: More on logarithmic sums of convex bodies\nAuthors: Christos Saroglou\nDate: Mon Sep 15 17:43:10 2014\n\nWe prove that the log-Brunn-Minkowski inequality (log-BMI) for the Lebesque measure in dimension $n$ would imply the log-BMI and, therefore, the B-conjecture for any log-concave density in dimension $n$. As a consequence, we prove the log-BMI and the B-conjecture for any log-concave density, in the plane. Moreover, we prove that the log-BMI reduces to the following: For each dimension $n$, there is a density $f_n$, which satisfies an integrability assumption, so that the log-BMI holds for parallelepipeds with parallel facets, for the density $f_n$. As byproduct of our methods, we study possible log-concavity of the function $t\\mapsto |(K+_p\\cdot e^tL)^{\\circ}|$, where $p\\geq 1$ and $K$, $L$ are symmetric convex bodies, which we are able to prove in some instances and as a further application, we confirm the variance conjecture in a special class of convex bodies. Finally, we establish a non-trivial dual form of the log-BMI.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA local uniqueness theorem for minimizers of Petty's conjectured projection inequality (https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.03796)\nciteturn1academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.0 years ago; Title: A local uniqueness theorem for minimizers of Petty's conjectured projection inequality ... Ivaki ... Here $K$ is an $n$-dimensional convex body, $\\Pi K$ is the projection body of $K$ and $\\Pi^2K=\\Pi(\\Pi K).\nTitle: A local uniqueness theorem for minimizers of Petty's conjectured projection inequality\nAuthors: Mohammad N. Ivaki\nDate: Wed Oct 12 17:33:36 2016\n\nEmploying the inverse function theorem on Banach spaces, we prove that in a $C^{2}(S^{n-1})$-neighborhood of the unit ball, the only solutions of $\\Pi^2K=cK$ are origin-centered ellipsoids. Here $K$ is an $n$-dimensional convex body, $\\Pi K$ is the projection body of $K$ and $\\Pi^2K=\\Pi(\\Pi K).$--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMORE ON LOGARITHMIC SUMS OF CONVEX BODIES - Saroglou - 2016 - Mathematika - Wiley Online Library (https://londmathsoc.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1112/S0025579316000061)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.4 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # MORE ON LOGARITHMIC SUMS OF CONVEX BODIES ...   * saroglou@math.tamu.edu ... We prove that the log-Brunn–Minkowski inequality (log-BMI) for the Lebesgue measure in dimension Image: urn:x-wiley:00255793:media:mtks0025579316000061:mtks0025579316000061-math-0001 would imply the log-BMI and, therefore, the B-conjecture for any even log-concave measure in dimension Image: urn:x-wiley:00255793:media:mtks0025579316000061:mtks0025579316000061-math-0002.\n\nVolume 62, Issue 3 pp. 818-841\n\nResearch Article\n\n# MORE ON LOGARITHMIC SUMS OF CONVEX BODIES\n\nChristos Saroglou, \n\nChristos Saroglou\n\n  * saroglou@math.tamu.edu\n  * christos.saroglou@gmail.com\n\nDepartment of Mathematics, Texas A&M University, College Station, TX, 77843 U.S.A.\n\nFirst published: 10 May 2016\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1112/S0025579316000061Digital Object Identifier (DOI)\n\n### Abstract\n\nWe prove that the log-Brunn–Minkowski inequality (log-BMI) for the Lebesgue measure in dimension Image: urn:x-wiley:00255793:media:mtks0025579316000061:mtks0025579316000061-math-0001 would imply the log-BMI and, therefore, the B-conjecture for any even log-concave measure in dimension Image: urn:x-wiley:00255793:media:mtks0025579316000061:mtks002